  Document classes remain one of the main barriers to accessible
  \LaTeX{} output. This article describes a template-based approach to
  class design in which the major structural elements of a
  document\Dash such as headings, lists, floats, captions, front matter,
  and page styles\Dash are all exposed through a consistent, tagging-aware
  interface.

  The same framework can emulate established customization packages
  such as \pkg{geometry}, \pkg{fancyhdr}, \pkg{titlesec},
  \pkg{titletoc}, and \pkg{enumitem}, making it possible to adapt both
  legacy and newly written classes without extensive manual
  intervention. The result is a more maintainable class architecture
  with accessibility built in from the beginning, building on the
  broader \LaTeX{} tagged-\PDF\ project.
